    formulaSource: "Morton RW et al. 2018. A systematic review, meta-analysis and meta-regression of the effect of protein supplementation on resistance training-induced gains in muscle mass and strength. Br J Sports Med.",
    explanation: "Protein is the most important macronutrient for body composition, serving as the building block for muscle tissue, enzymes, hormones, and immune factors. Unlike carbohydrates and fats, the body has no dedicated storage depot for protein — excess is either used for energy or converted to fat. The minimum protein recommendation of 0.8 g/kg/day (from the WHO/RDA) was set to prevent deficiency, not to optimize muscle mass or health in active individuals. Research consistently shows that active adults benefit from substantially more protein — 1.6–2.0 g/kg/day — particularly when engaging in resistance training. During calorie restriction for weight loss, higher protein intake (1.8–2.4 g/kg) is especially important to preserve lean muscle mass, which keeps metabolism elevated and improves body composition outcomes. The body's ability to use protein for muscle protein synthesis appears to plateau around 2.2 g/kg for most people (excluding some elite strength athletes), so intakes significantly above this offer diminishing returns. Protein timing also matters: distributing intake across three to five meals, with 25–40 g per meal, maximizes muscle protein synthesis throughout the day rather than consuming most protein in one sitting.",
    example: {
      inputs: { system: "metric", weight: 80, goal: "gain" },
      explanation: "An 80 kg person building muscle needs 128–160 g/day (optimal range), with a minimum of 128 g and upper range of 192 g.",
    },
    faqs: [
      { question: "Can you eat too much protein?", answer: "For healthy adults, high protein intakes up to 2.5–3.0 g/kg body weight appear safe based on available evidence and don't damage healthy kidneys. However, individuals with pre-existing kidney disease should limit protein intake as directed by their physician, since damaged kidneys struggle to process protein metabolites. Very high protein intakes (above 3 g/kg) provide no additional muscle-building benefit and crowd out other nutrients. Additionally, high protein from processed meat sources carries independent health risks — lean meats, fish, legumes, and dairy are superior sources." },
      { question: "What are the best protein sources?", answer: "Complete proteins (containing all essential amino acids) include: animal sources (chicken, turkey, beef, fish, eggs, dairy) and some plant sources (soy, quinoa, hemp). Incomplete plant proteins (beans, rice, lentils, nuts) can be combined throughout the day to get all essential amino acids — you don't need to combine them in every meal. For muscle building, leucine-rich proteins (whey, eggs, beef) have the strongest muscle protein synthesis signal. For health more broadly, research supports emphasizing fish, legumes, and poultry over processed red meats." },
      { question: "When should I eat protein for best results?", answer: "Protein timing matters but is secondary to total daily intake. Research supports consuming 25–40 g of protein per meal to maximize per-meal muscle protein synthesis — amounts much below 20 g are less effective, and amounts above 40 g are used for energy rather than muscle building. For resistance training, consuming protein within 2 hours post-workout supports recovery and muscle adaptation, though the window is wider than once believed. A protein-rich breakfast helps manage hunger throughout the day. Most practically: distribute protein intake across three to five meals rather than concentrating it in one or two large meals." },
      { question: "Do plant-based eaters need more protein?", answer: "Plant proteins generally have lower digestibility and different amino acid profiles than animal proteins, which may reduce the effective protein available for muscle protein synthesis. Research suggests plant-based athletes may benefit from targeting the upper end of protein recommendations (1.8–2.2 g/kg) and ensuring adequate leucine intake (found in soy, legumes, and nuts). Well-planned plant-based diets that combine diverse protein sources can absolutely support muscle building — many elite plant-based athletes demonstrate this. Soy protein in particular has a protein quality score comparable to whey." },
      { question: "What about protein powders — are they necessary?", answer: "Protein powders are convenient but not necessary if you can meet your protein target through whole foods. They are particularly useful when: whole food options are limited (travel, busy schedules), appetite is low but protein needs are high, or post-workout intake is needed quickly. Whey protein (from milk) is the best-studied and most rapidly absorbed; casein (also from milk) absorbs slowly and is useful before sleep. Plant-based powders (pea, rice, soy blend) are comparable in muscle-building efficacy to whey when matched for leucine content. Look for third-party tested products (Informed Sport, NSF Certified) to ensure label accuracy." },
    ],
