    formulaExplanation: "The MET (Metabolic Equivalent of Task) is a standardized measure of activity intensity: 1 MET equals the energy expended at rest. Walking briskly is about 4 METs; running vigorously is 11–14 METs. The Ainsworth MET formula converts MET, body weight, and duration into calories: (MET × 3.5 × kg / 200) × minutes gives calories per minute. Total calories is calories per minute times duration.",
    formulaSource: "Ainsworth BE et al., 2011. Compendium of Physical Activities. Med Sci Sports Exerc.",
    explanation: "The Metabolic Equivalent of Task (MET) system is the scientific standard for quantifying the energy cost of physical activities. Each activity has an assigned MET value based on measured oxygen consumption during that activity relative to rest (1 MET). This allows a universal formula to estimate calorie burn from any activity using just body weight and duration — no need for expensive metabolic testing equipment. The formula systematically accounts for the fact that heavier individuals burn more calories doing the same activity (more mass to move) and that higher-intensity activities (higher MET) burn proportionally more. Important caveats: all calorie burn estimates have substantial individual variation (±20–30%) due to differences in fitness level, movement efficiency, and metabolism. Exercise equipment (treadmills, ellipticals) tend to overestimate calorie burn by 10–30%. The afterburn effect (EPOC — excess post-exercise oxygen consumption) after intense exercise adds additional calories not captured here but is often modest (50–200 cal) outside very high-intensity sessions. These estimates are best used for relative comparisons between activities and for rough planning, not as precise measurements.",
